# Two cases of one variant may not share a name, whatever their payloads: a
# constructor names a case, so `Whoops` would be ambiguous between the two. The
# error points at the second one, the case that repeats a name already taken.
#
# Its own fixture, not a line in `err_variants.aipl`: a duplicate case is a
# *parse* error, and a file that fails to parse reports nothing else, so it would
# have hidden every type error that fixture exists to check.

variant Invalid = Whoops(i64) | Whoops

fn main() {}
--- errors ---
error: duplicate case "Whoops"
 --> ./tests/cases/variants/err_duplicate_case.aipl:9:33
  |
9 | variant Invalid = Whoops(i64) | Whoops
  |                                 ^^^^^^
